Source: https://codereview.qt-project.org/c/qt/qtbase/+/777426 (patch set 1, under review, not merged)

From 730020b0d64b4dbde7877889c31c4f4f19adf67e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Maxim Chernobayev <kuzmeech@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 03 Oct 2026 23:39:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Widgets a11y: survive cell interfaces deleted behind the table's back

QAccessibleTable keeps the interfaces of the cells it handed out in
childToId and assumes it is the only one deleting them. That is not
true on macOS: when the cocoa plugin drops its row/column element
arrays (removeElementsFromCache:) it calls QAccessibleCache::deleteInterface
for every proper cell, so the table ends up with ids that no longer
resolve. The next RowsInserted/RowsRemoved event then dereferences a
null interface in modelChange() (the Q_ASSERT is compiled out in
release), and child() hands out nullptr for a perfectly valid cell
in between. Seen as a SIGSEGV in qBittorrent's transfer list as soon
as any accessibility client has walked the window once.

Make the table tolerate this: skip stale ids when rebuilding the cache
in modelChange(), and treat a stale id in child() as a cache miss so
the cell is simply created again. Same for the tree and list variants.

This complements the cocoa side change for QTBUG-149612 (which stops
the synthesized elements from deleting the parent table) and keeps the
widgets side safe regardless of what the platform plugin does with the
cache.

Task-number: QTBUG-149612
Pick-to: 6.12 6.11 6.10 6.8
Change-Id: Ie9f21972875973a0962e49d212a8cbc5064849d1
